# The PROXY protocol from trusted gateways (server/README.md "Gateways and the PROXY protocol", docs/LAUNCH.md "Gateways
# and DDoS"). Owner decision: "put game proxies in front of the game server, and add per-IP connection limits."
# Players connect to TCP proxies (server/deploy/gateway/: HAProxy with send-proxy-v2) that forward 7171 and 7172 here;
# the real server's address is never published. The engine must still see each player's own address: bans, the
# per-IP connection throttle, the status query limit, the 2-characters-per-IP rule (player:getIp(), multiclient.lua),
# the client guard's staff IPs and every log use Connection::getIP.
#   * config.lua: proxyProtocolTrusted = "a.b.c.d,e.f.g.h/nn" (the gateways; IPv4 or a CIDR range; empty: off, every
#     connection is direct as before) and proxyProtocolRequired = false. Re-read after /reload config.
#   * ServicePort::onAccept: a connection from a trusted address must start with a PROXY header, v2 (binary) or v1
#     (text). Connection::readProxyHeader reads it in steps (8 bytes, then exactly what the parser asks for), never a
#     byte of the client's own data; 5 s to deliver it. Its source address becomes Connection::getIP. Only then do the
#     ban / throttle checks (on the real address) and the protocol start (ServicePort::onConnectionReady, the old
#     tail of onAccept), so the game port's challenge goes out after the header. LOCAL headers (a gateway's health
#     check), UNSPEC and v1 UNKNOWN keep the gateway's address; a gateway's own address skips the per-IP throttle (the
#     gateways limit their clients themselves). IPv6 clients are refused (the engine stores IPv4 only; an IPv4-mapped
#     IPv6 address is taken as IPv4). A trusted address without a valid header is closed with a console line.
#   * from any other address a PROXY header is never believed: a first packet that starts with "\r\n" or "PR" (lengths
#     no real first packet has) is refused with a console line.
#   * proxyProtocolRequired = true: every address that is not a trusted gateway is refused at accept, except
#     127.0.0.0/8 (the watchdog and the health check ask the status protocol on 127.0.0.1). The real server then cannot
#     be played directly even when its firewall is wrong.
#   * console lines are limited to one per kind every 10 s (a flood cannot fill the log); startup prints the mode.
